    "id": "fae-succession-rules",
    "name": "Fae Succession Rules",
    "category": "Customs",
    "body": "Succession among the Fae is determined by bloodline, recognition, legitimacy, and ancient law, not simply birth order.\n\nThe firstborn child is not automatically heir. A successor must be formally acknowledged by the ruling house and accepted under the territory’s succession customs.\n\nChildren born outside a formal union may still inherit if recognized by the ruling parent, but their claim may be challenged by noble houses or rival bloodlines.\n\nA ruler may name an heir from among their children, siblings, nieces, nephews, or other close blood relatives if the chosen successor meets the court’s legal requirements.\n\nPowerful noble houses may influence succession through alliances, marriage, political pressure, or formal objection, but they cannot simply appoint a ruler themselves.\n\nIf no clear heir exists, succession may be decided by blood-right, council recognition, magical trial, ancient oath, or challenge, depending on the territory.\n\nIn Varrinth, the High Lord’s chosen heir must ultimately be recognized by House Veyr, witnessed before the court, and accepted by Varrinth’s old magic.",
